#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101678} :activity/id #uuid "d19bc789-6fc7-5102-bc7b-8f270f9b39a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101959 :tutorial/content "When multiplying two matrices in general, we apply the same approach as usual:\n\n- First, we verify that the two matrices are conformable for multiplication.\n\n- Then, we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second.\n\nFor example, let's find $AB,$ where \n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we verify the conformability for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{3}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{B} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{C}\n$$\n\nSo the matrices are conformable for multiplication, and the product is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let's set up the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        -2 & 2 & 1  \\\\\n\t1 & -1 & 3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\n\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        -5 & 2   \\\\\n\t2 & -2   \\\\\n\t-4 & 1 \n    \\end{bmatrix}}_{B}\n\n\t&= \n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\ast & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\t}_{C}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product matrix (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $c_{11},$ the entry at the intersection of the $1$st row and $1$st column, we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the $1$st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{blue}-2 & \\color{blue}2 & \\color{blue}1  \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}1 & \\color{lightgray}-1 & \\color{lightgray}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{lightgray}2   \\\\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{lightgray}-2   \\\\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{lightgray}1\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n\\%\t&= \n\\%    \\left[\n\\%   \\begin{matrix}\n\\%       ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}}) & \\ast   \\\\\n\\%        \\ast & \\ast\n\\%    \\end{matrix}\n\\%    \\right]\n\n\t= \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} = ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{12},$ we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the $2$nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{blue}-2 & \\color{blue}2 & \\color{blue}1  \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}1 & \\color{lightgray}-1 & \\color{lightgray}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{lightgray}-5 & \\color{red}2   \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{red}-2   \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n\\%\t&= \n\\%    \\left[\n\\%    \\begin{matrix}\n\\%        10 & ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}   \\\\\n\\%        \\ast & \\ast\n\\%    \\end{matrix}\n\\%    \\right]\n\n\t= \\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7}   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7} = ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{21},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the $1$st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{lightgray}-2 & \\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{lightgray}1  \\\\\n\t\\color{blue}1 & \\color{blue}-1 & \\color{blue}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{lightgray}2   \\\\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{lightgray}-2   \\\\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{lightgray}1\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n\\%\t&= \n\\%    \\left[\n\\%    \\begin{matrix}\n\\%        10 & -7   \\\\\n\\%        {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}}) & \\ast\n\\%    \\end{matrix}\n\\%    \\right]\n\n\t= \\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & -7   \\\\\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find $c_{22},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the $2$nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{lightgray}-2 & \\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{lightgray}1  \\\\\n\t\\color{blue}1 & \\color{blue}-1 & \\color{blue}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{lightgray}-5 & \\color{red}2   \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{red}-2   \\\\\n\t\\color{lightgray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n\\%\t&= \n\\%    \\left[\n\\%    \\begin{matrix}\n\\%        10 & -7   \\\\\n\\%        -19 & {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n\\%    \\end{matrix}\n\\%    \\right]\n\n\t= \\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & -7   \\\\\n        -19 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7}\n    \\end{bmatrix}, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7} = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 \\\\ -19 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce402944-0109-5d9d-93d5-394323fbc469" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2993 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "190a2922-1a7b-53bc-995b-5efd5caf84b2" :step/math-academy-id 16186 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100694} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92dd864a-8bc0-547c-84c9-ffd562909315" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048783}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338850}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Two Matrices When They Are Conformable"} :step/id #uuid "864b056d-3d24-5ade-a901-89324e218c5c" :step/math-academy-id 16187 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101960 :tutorial/content "It's possible to express matrix multiplication using index notation.\n\nTo see how, first let $C=AB,$ where $A$ and $B$ are two matrices that are conformable for multiplication.\n\nThen $c_{\\color{red}{i}\\color{blue}{j}}$ represents the entry at the intersection of the $\\color{red}i$th row and $\\color{blue}j$th column in $C.$\n\nTo compute $c_{\\color{red}{i}\\color{blue}{j}},$ we take the dot product of the $\\color{red}i$th row of $\\color{red}A$ and the $\\color{blue}j$th column of ${\\color{blue}B},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{i1} & {a}_{i2} & ⋯ & {a}_{in}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b8d43b0-7ded-5276-9ed1-766d5f5fff93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2994 :tutorial/title "Index Notation of Matrix Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "bc9f1c5d-87d6-5b08-95da-74e0d5064eae" :step/math-academy-id 16188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100695} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71ff07eb-2e16-529e-b17c-1bf7cb9703bd" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521964} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338856}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of the Product of Two Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "09c42d8b-5f21-5e0f-b672-291ed8dc0ef2" :step/math-academy-id 16189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100741} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d7d67c6-d54f-5ee1-af59-fd63d6687cfd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396025}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Column-Vector By a Row-Vec